findings.body:
The helper escapes `%` and `_` with a backslash, but the Iris fallback declares `ESCAPE '|'`. Under that clause, a backslash is an ordinary character. A keyword like `test_data` becomes `%test\_data%`, which matches `test\` followed by any character and misses the literal `test_data` text. Keywords containing `%` or `_` regress from working wildcard-free matches to returning nothing. Pure-alphanumeric queries are unaffected.

::diff{file="api/core/rag/datasource/vdb/iris/iris_vector.py" lines="289-300"}

Use the same backslash escape character the helper emits, for example `ESCAPE '\\'` with the SQL-literal form the IRIS driver expects. Add a unit test that asserts the generated SQL and parameter together for a keyword containing `_`.

The original code converted the keyword with `encode('unicode_escape')` and rewrote `\u` to `\\u`, so a Chinese keyword matched the `\uXXXX` form that `json.dumps` writes into `WorkflowRun.inputs`/`outputs`. The PR removes that step entirely. Now `"你好"` is searched as raw characters, but the stored JSON contains escape sequences, so the match fails. This is a behavioral change the PR does not mention, and no test covers non-ASCII keywords.

::diff{file="api/services/workflow_app_service.py" lines="89-96"}

Keep the unicode-escape transform and apply the LIKE escape on top of it, in that order: convert to the stored JSON form first, then call `escape_like_pattern`. Add an integration test that searches a non-ASCII keyword against a run whose inputs were stored via `json.dumps`.

Every other case in this `match` passes `escape="\\"`, but `not contains` doesn't. The pattern is escaped, so the result is correct only because Postgres and MySQL default their LIKE escape to backslash. On any backend without that default, `not contains` silently matches the wrong rows. It also breaks the local convention that every escaped pattern names its escape character.

::diff{file="api/core/rag/retrieval/dataset_retrieval.py" lines="1205-1208"}

Add `escape="\\"` to the `notlike` call.

The PR title and description say LIKE escaping is applied "across all database queries," but two document-name search paths still interpolate raw input: the console document list (`controllers/console/datasets/datasets_document.py`, around lines 264-266) and the service-API document list (`controllers/service_api/dataset/document.py`, around lines 474-476). Both wrap the input in `%...%` with no escaping and no escape clause. Anyone who fixes the other sites will reasonably assume these are covered, so a follow-up is likely to miss them.

::diff{file="api/services/dataset_service.py" lines="146-148"}

Apply the same `escape_like_pattern` + `escape="\\"` treatment to both document search sites, or narrow the PR title and description to what's actually covered.

The helper doubles backslashes, then the Clickzetta code does `.replace("'", "''")` and writes `ESCAPE '\\\\'` in the Python f-string, which produces `ESCAPE '\\'` in the SQL. This is correct only if Clickzetta's string literal does not interpret backslashes as escapes. If it does (as MySQL does), the escaped pattern is decoded twice: `\\%` becomes `\%` at the literal level and the LIKE sees a different escape sequence. The PR adds no integration test against Clickzetta, so this can't be confirmed from the diff.

::diff{file="api/core/rag/datasource/vdb/clickzetta/clickzetta_vector.py" lines="987-991"}

Confirm Clickzetta's string-literal rules for backslash, and either add a test that asserts the exact SQL for a keyword containing `\\`, `%` and `_`, or build the literal from a parameter if the driver supports it.

Several new integration tests claim to verify that `50%` does not match `100% different`. The unescaped pattern `%50%%` requires the substring `50`, and `100% different` doesn't contain it, so the row is excluded whether or not escaping works. The assertions pass on the original, unescaped code, so they can't catch the bug they document. The workflow test's `>= 1` assertions have the same issue.

::code{file="api/tests/test_containers_integration_tests/services/test_tag_service.py" lines="388-392"}

Make the negative case discriminating: use a row that contains the same characters the wildcard would match, for example `1000% off` for keyword `10%`, or `a_c` alongside `abc` for `_`. Then assert the exact result set rather than `>= 1`.

`test_app_service.py` and `test_workflow_app_service.py` comment out the module-level `AppService` import and re-import it inside roughly 20 test functions, citing a "circular dependency." Nothing in the diff shows a cycle: `libs/helper.py` doesn't import services, and `services/app_service.py` imports fine at module scope elsewhere. If there is a real collection-time problem, it should be fixed or documented with the actual cycle. Otherwise this is churn that makes the file harder to read and hides the import from linters.

::code{file="api/tests/test_containers_integration_tests/services/test_app_service.py" lines="11-12"}

Restore the top-level import. If a cycle really does exist, document the cycle in a comment that names the modules involved.

The helper is imported inside the function body in at least six service and controller functions, but `services/dataset_service.py` already uses the module-level `from libs import helper` and calls `helper.escape_like_pattern`. Two conventions for the same helper make the code harder to scan. Function-level imports are only justified by a real cycle, and the PR gives no evidence of one.

::diff{file="api/services/tag_service.py" lines="19-26"}

Import the helper at module scope, following the `from libs import helper` pattern in `dataset_service.py`.

The helper's signature is `pattern: str -> str`, but `if not pattern: return pattern` returns `None` for `None` input, and a unit test asserts that. Either the type hint or the guard is wrong. Also, the local variable is named `escapedPattern` in camelCase, which breaks the snake_case used elsewhere in `libs/helper.py`.

::code{file="api/libs/helper.py" lines="35-70"}

Rename the local to `escaped_pattern`. Decide whether `None` is allowed: if not, drop the guard and let an empty string flow through; if it is, type the parameter as `str | None`.

The helper is a small pure function with a clear docstring, and it escapes backslash before `%` and `_`, so it doesn't double-escape. `TestEscapeLikePattern` covers each special character, mixed input, empty input, and the ordering rule. Most SQLAlchemy call sites pass `escape="\\"` explicitly, so the escape character is visible where the query is built.

::file{file="api/libs/helper.py"}

Keep this pattern. The remaining issues are at the call sites that drifted from it.

The PR adds container-backed integration tests for annotations, apps, tags, and workflow logs. That covers the affected services end to end, which the unit tests can't. The main gap is the weak negative assertions (see the finding on that), and the Iris and Clickzetta raw-SQL paths have no tests at all.

::file{file="api/tests/test_containers_integration_tests/services/test_annotation_service.py"}

Add coverage for the Iris and Clickzetta raw-SQL paths so the escape-clause mismatch above would be caught.
